# quinn-netem decision-log v1 seq direction verdict cause wire_bytes now_ns release_ns dup_release_ns corrupt_offset corrupt_bit queue_backlog_bytes
#
# ==========================================================================
#  THE GATE FIXTURE.  DERIVING THIS FILE BY RUNNING THE IMPLEMENTATION IS
#  FORBIDDEN.  Every record below was written from the arithmetic in this
#  header and from nothing else.
# ==========================================================================
#
#  A fixture blessed from the code under test certifies whatever that code
#  does, including nothing at all.  An engine whose decide function returned
#  "pass" unconditionally would bless ten thousand records from itself and
#  turn the whole reproducibility criterion green.  Comparing an
#  implementation against its own output proves only that it equals itself.
#
#  So if a line here disagrees with the engine, the engine and this table
#  disagree, and finding out which one is wrong is the work.  Re-derive the
#  line from the rules below.  Do not paste what the engine printed.
#
#  Comment lines (first non-blank byte `#`) and blank lines are dropped from
#  BOTH sides of the comparison, so this header costs the byte comparison
#  nothing and travels in the same file as the records it explains.
#
#  --- the profile these records come from ---------------------------------
#
#  One direction, Downlink, so column 2 is 0 on every line.  seed 7.
#  100 datagrams, seq 0..99, one per millisecond: now_ns = seq * 1000000.
#
#  Sizes are ON-WIRE bytes.  Every datagram is 1228 (a 1200-byte payload plus
#  20 bytes of IPv4 header and 8 of UDP) except:
#      seq 5, 23, 37, 88  ->  1500   (over the MTU limit)
#      seq 40             ->  1400   (exactly the limit; the limit passes)
#
#  Armed on every phase:
#      rate           bps 8000000, burst_bytes 1500, queue_bytes 2000
#      mtu_blackhole  1400
#
#  A timeline swaps the rest of the profile twice:
#
#    phase A, ticks [0, 50ms)      seq 0..49
#        loss       Pattern, indices [3, 7, 11, 22, 29, 44]
#        blackouts  [ at_ns 20ms, for_ns 5ms ]
#        delay, reorder, dup, corrupt: none
#    phase B, at_ns 50ms           seq 50..79
#        loss       EveryNth n = 5
#        delay      Fixed 2ms
#        reorder    gap 4, p = 1
#        dup        p = 1
#        corrupt: none.  blackouts: none.
#    phase C, at_ns 80ms           seq 80..99
#        phase B plus corrupt p = 1
#
#  Every probability in the profile is exactly 0 or exactly 1, so no verdict
#  on any line depends on the value of a generator draw.  The reorder and
#  duplication models still consume one draw each per datagram they judge —
#  that is what keeps a later model's stream in the position it would be in
#  anyway — but at p = 1 the answer is certain and the arithmetic below is
#  complete without it.
#
#  --- the rules, in the order the engine applies them ---------------------
#
#  1. TIMELINE.  The phase is the last step whose at_ns is <= now.
#  2. BLACKOUT (phase A only).  Half-open: 20ms <= now < 25ms, i.e. seq
#     20..24 inclusive and seq 25 NOT.  Drop, cause 2.  Consumes no draw and
#     never touches the queue, so the queue's backlog and its drain clock are
#     exactly where the last admitted datagram left them.
#  3. MTU.  Strictly greater than 1400 drops, cause 3.  So 1500 drops and the
#     1400 at seq 40 passes.  No draw, no queue.
#  4. LOSS.  Phase A: the named index list.  Phases B and C: seq % 5 == 4,
#     i.e. the 5th, 10th, ... datagram.  Drop, cause 1.  No draw either way —
#     both of these loss models are deterministic in seq.
#  5. RATE, and it is two separate pieces of arithmetic.
#
#     THE QUEUE, in bytes.  It drains at the configured rate, 8000000 bits/s
#     = 1000000 bytes/s = exactly 1 byte per microsecond, so a 1ms gap drains
#     1000 bytes.  Drain first, floored at zero, then admit:
#
#         backlog := max(0, backlog - microseconds_since_the_last_ADMISSION
#                                     _ATTEMPT)
#         if backlog + wire_bytes > 2000 -> drop, cause 4, backlog unchanged
#         else                            -> backlog := backlog + wire_bytes
#
#     Column 11 is the backlog AFTER this decision, this datagram included.
#     A datagram dropped at step 2, 3 or 4 never reaches the queue, so it
#     neither drains it nor is counted in it, and its column 11 repeats the
#     previous admitted line's.  A tail drop DOES drain first, so its column
#     11 is the post-drain backlog and is never the pre-drain one.
#
#     THE BUCKET, also in bytes, and it is what column 7 is about.  Depth
#     1500, refilling at the same 1 byte per microsecond, and it starts FULL.
#     Only a granted datagram spends from it.  Writing t_g for the tick of
#     the last granted datagram and s_g for that datagram's size:
#
#         held(now) = min(1500, 1500 - s_g + microseconds_since_t_g)
#         held >= this datagram's size  -> release = now
#         otherwise                     -> release = now + (size - held) us
#
#     With 1228-byte datagrams one millisecond apart that works out to two
#     grants and then one deferral: the first two lines are granted, and the
#     third is short by 184 bytes and is released 184000 ns late.  Every
#     `+184000` in column 7 below is that number, and the single `+356000` at
#     seq 40 is the same arithmetic for the 1400-byte datagram there.
#
#  6. DELAY (phases B and C).  Fixed 2ms, added to whatever tick step 5 named.
#     A fixed delay consumes no draw.
#  7. REORDER (phases B and C).  Every 4th datagram is a candidate:
#     seq % 4 == 3.  A candidate that hits — and at p = 1 every candidate
#     hits — is released at `now` instead, and its verdict is 2, not 0.  The
#     distinction is the whole visible effect of the model: the datagram did
#     not wait, where a 0 would say it was never asked to.
#  8. DUPLICATION (phases B and C).  p = 1, so every datagram that gets this
#     far gets a copy, released at the same tick.  Column 8 is therefore
#     equal to column 7 on those lines, and 18446744073709551615 (u64::MAX,
#     "no duplicate") everywhere else.
#  9. CORRUPTION (phase C).  p = 1.  Columns 9 and 10 are the byte offset and
#     the bit, and they are THE ONLY TWO COLUMNS IN THIS FILE THAT COME FROM
#     THE GENERATOR RATHER THAN FROM ARITHMETIC.  4294967295 (u32::MAX) in
#     column 9 means no corruption.
#
#     The corruption stream is PCG32 seeded (state 7, sequence 4 — the
#     downlink corruption stream id), and it is consumed three words per
#     corrupted datagram: one for the hit, one for the offset, one for the
#     bit.  The offset is bounded by wire_bytes - 48, which is 1180 here, and
#     the bit by 8.  The bounded draw rejects below (-bound) mod bound, which
#     is 936 for bound 1180 and 0 for bound 8; every offset word listed below
#     is >= 936, so no draw in this file was rejected and each offset is
#     simply `word mod 1180` and each bit `word mod 8`.
#
#     The three words per datagram, so a reader can check columns 9 and 10
#     against any independent PCG32 rather than against this crate's:
#
#        seq    hit word    offset word   bit word    -> offset  bit
#         80   1919132652    1862406582   2363703883       782    3
#         81   3850697976    3945852795   3695547696        55    0
#         82   1257919033    1362110348    265285222       948    6
#         83   4148610212     491602704   2560990673       544    1
#         85   3956148511     624063629   2491204434       569    2
#         86      1252320    2395427962    138443112       822    0
#         87   2341817487    4197356266    366435041       686    1
#         90   4016944071    3496535293     60916133       593    5
#         91     27858644    3072413651    142539314       451    2
#         92   3951733527    1708008331   3344568846       811    6
#         93    808414341    1324557222   3999081807       142    7
#         95   3370822031    1878265947   1560657650       947    2
#         96    860925615     738478481   2859278908       261    4
#         97   2516280836    2770122128   3088559434       148    2
#         98   1395614435    2554923119    634634759        99    7
#
#     Those words were produced by a second transcription of the generator,
#     written from the published algorithm, and it is trustworthy for one
#     specific reason: it reproduces the generator's published demo vector
#     (seeded(42, 54) -> a15c02b7 7b47f409 ba1d3330 83d2f293) and the pinned
#     first word of the all-zero seed (e4c14788) before it is used for
#     anything here.  A number an independent anchored implementation agrees
#     on is evidence.  A number the code under test printed is not.
#
#  --- what happened to which datagram, so a reader can spot-check ----------
#
#     dropped, loss (cause 1)      3, 7, 11, 29, 44, and then 54, 59, 64, 69,
#                                  74, 79, 84, 89, 94, 99
#     dropped, blackout (cause 2)  20, 21, 22, 23, 24
#     dropped, MTU (cause 3)       5, 37, 88
#     dropped, queue full (4)      16, 34, 41, 49
#     bucket named a later tick    2, 10, 14, 19, 27, 32, 40, 47, 52, 57, 62,
#                                  67, 72, 77, 82, 87, 92, 97.  At 67 and 87
#                                  the reorder model then took the release
#                                  back to `now`, so those two lines do not
#                                  show the lateness in column 7.
#     reordered (verdict 2)        51, 55, 63, 67, 71, 75, 83, 87, 91, 95
#     corrupted                    80..99 less 84, 88, 89, 94 and 99, which
#                                  were dropped before step 9
#
#  TWO PRECEDENCE FACTS ARE PINNED HERE ON PURPOSE, because they are the ones
#  an implementation gets wrong silently:
#
#     seq 22 is in the loss index list AND inside the blackout.  It is
#     reported as cause 2, not cause 1, because the blackout is consulted
#     first.  If it ever reads 1, the model order moved.
#     seq 23 is over the MTU limit AND inside the blackout.  Same answer, for
#     the same reason.
#
#  And one boundary each way: seq 19 is one millisecond before the blackout
#  and survives; seq 25 is the first millisecond after it and survives, which
#  is what makes the window half-open rather than five-and-a-bit long.
#
#  Counts, as a cheap whole-file check: 100 records; 26 with verdict 0, 37
#  with verdict 1, 10 with verdict 2, 27 with verdict 3; and of the 27 drops,
#  15 loss, 5 blackout, 3 MTU, 4 queue-full.
#
